\section{The simulated datasets}{
Each dataset has a new surface, new values of the nugget and the random
effects, and new responses at the observed locations, with the observed
covariates, offsets and denominators. The parameters stay at their
estimates. \code{\link[=simulate_glgpm]{simulate_glgpm()}} simulates them.

The surface is simulated again, and not predicted from the data, so that the
model must reproduce a feature through its structure. A surface that is
conditioned on the data has adapted to them. Datasets simulated from it
resemble the observed one more than the model itself does, so a check of them
can miss what is wrong with the model.
}

\section{Statistics}{
By default they are the mean and the standard deviation of the empirical
prevalence or rate and the proportion of zeros (\code{mean_prevalence},
\code{sd_prevalence} and \code{zeros}, or \code{mean_rate}, \code{sd_rate} and \code{zeros}), and for
Gaussian models the mean, the standard deviation, the minimum and the maximum
of the response (\code{mean}, \code{sd}, \code{min} and \code{max}). They are those of the
observations for Gaussian, Poisson and negative binomial models. For binomial
models they are those of the locations, where the positives and the
denominators are added up first, because the prevalence of a single
individual is only 0 or 1.

A function in \code{statistics} can calculate anything from a dataset, such as a
correlation with a covariate, and can return a vector of named numbers, a
list of them or a data frame with one row. A function in a named list that
returns one number gives it the name of the list. Simulated datasets in
which a statistic isn't finite are left out of its summary, with a warning.
}

\section{Distribution and variogram}{
The distribution is that of the same values as the default statistics, the
prevalence of each location, or the rate or response of each observation.

The variogram is that of the residuals from the covariates. The response is
first put on the scale of the linear predictor, as the empirical logit for
binomial models, the logarithm of the rate with 0.5 added to the count for
models of counts, and the response itself for Gaussian models. It has one
value for each location, where the observations are added up, or averaged
for Gaussian models. The residuals are the differences between these values,
less any offset, and their least squares fit on the covariates of the model.
The regression is done again for each simulated dataset. The variogram of
the values themselves would be dominated by a strong trend, which the
covariates explain, and would show little of the spatial correlation. Each
model has its own residuals and so its own observed variogram.

The joint envelope is drawn as in \code{\link[=variogram]{variogram()}}, from the observed and the
simulated curves ordered by their extreme rank lengths, at a level of 95\%.
With the parameters at their true values, the whole observed curve is inside
it with a probability of at least 95\%.
}

\section{What a check can tell}{
A feature far outside what the model simulates is one that the model misses.
The features that tell most are those that the model has no parameter for. A
feature that the model was fitted to, such as the mean, can hardly fail,
whatever is wrong with the rest of the model.

A model that passes is not shown to be right. The parameters are estimated
from the same data, so the simulated datasets are centred near the observed
one, and the check is conservative. A correct model is outside an envelope
less often than 5\% of the time. With few locations, or when the spatial
correlation extends over much of the study area, datasets simulated from the
same model differ a lot from each other, and the check has little power.

The edge of an envelope depends on the simulations, so that another \code{seed}
moves it, and a single point near the edge, on either side of it, says
little. The first distance classes often have few pairs, so a single point
outside the envelope there needs care.

Nothing here is a p-value or a mark that a model passes or fails. The
proportion of simulated values at or above the observed one says where it
lies among them.
}

